---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="You are not alone"
  description="Plain language information and real community support for women with pelvic organ prolapse."
  current="/"
>
  <div class="hero">
    <h1>You are not alone in this. And you never were.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Up to half of women who have given birth have some degree of pelvic organ
      prolapse. Most were never told it could happen. This site exists to give
      you clear information, honest answers, and a community of women who
      understand.
    </p>
    <div class="ornament" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></div>
  </div>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="label">Where to begin</span>
      <h2>Start wherever you are</h2>
      <p>
        Three ways in, depending on what brought you here today.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="doors doors--3 wrap">
      <li>
        <a class="door" href="/symptoms/">
          <span class="whisper">Start here if</span>
          <h2>Something feels wrong and you cannot name it</h2>
          <p>
            What prolapse commonly feels like, plus a private two minute self
            check to help you decide whether to talk to someone.
          </p>
          <span class="go">Symptoms and self check</span>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="door" href="/understanding/">
          <span class="whisper">Start here if</span>
          <h2>You have just been diagnosed</h2>
          <p>
            What is happening inside your body, the five types of prolapse, and
            every treatment path, explained without jargon or alarm.
          </p>
          <span class="go">Understanding prolapse</span>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="door" href="/treatment/surgery/">
          <span class="whisper">Start here if</span>
          <h2>Surgery is ahead of you, or behind you</h2>
          <p>
            Reconstructive and obliterative surgery explained, realistic
            recovery expectations, and honest answers about recurrence.
          </p>
          <span class="go">Surgery, recovery and recurrence</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="label">The short version</span>
      <h2>What is pelvic organ prolapse?</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      Prolapse happens when the muscles and tissues that hold up your pelvic
      organs weaken or get damaged. The bladder, uterus, rectum, or vagina can
      then shift downward and press into the vaginal wall. Women describe it as
      heaviness, pressure, a dragging feeling, or a bulge.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It is common, it is treatable, and it is nothing to be ashamed
      of.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="actions" style="margin-top:1.8rem;">
      <a class="btn btn--quiet" href="/understanding/">Learn what is happening</a>
    </p>

    <p class="pullquote">
      Prolapse has been documented in medical texts for nearly four thousand
      years. It is time we started talking about it out loud.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
